#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Runs a neochumby tree on a Linux PC as if it were a Chumby Classic: the
static ARM binaries, unchanged, under qemu-arm user mode, chrooted in a fake
root with the Chumby's paths and fake devices. It tests the session and the
apps end to end without a Chumby. CONTRIBUTING.md says what it can't catch.

Needs: Linux; root (it mounts, and makes namespaces); Python 3.9+; FUSE
(/dev/fuse); util-linux's mount, and loop devices for a stick image;
qemu-user's binfmt handler for ARM, statically linked and registered with the
F flag, as Debian and Ubuntu do (`apt install qemu-user-binfmt`, or
qemu-user-static on older releases); dosfstools for `mkstick`.

  sudo dev/emulate start [TREE]   boots TREE's session (a tools/tree OUT)
         [--stick IMG|DIR]        a FAT image loop-mounted at /mnt/usb, or a
                                  directory; with no TREE, the session is
                                  the stick's neochumby/neochumby
         [--server ADDR[:PORT]]   a neochumbyd, as the boot hook passes it on
         [--psp DIR]              /psp starts as a copy of DIR
         [--env KEY=VALUE]        more environment for the session
         [--run PATH]             what to run, in the fake root, if not the
                                  session
         [--bind DIR:PATH]        DIR, read-only, at PATH in the fake root
                                  (e.g. pictures at /tmp/classic)
  dev/emulate tap X Y [--hold MS] a touch at screen pixel (X, Y)
  dev/emulate button down|up|press  the top button (/dev/switch)
  dev/emulate shot OUT.png        the screen (dev/fb2png.py)
  dev/emulate pixel X Y           one pixel, as #rrggbb
  dev/emulate compare [--min F] [--wait SECS] [--want RE] RAW ...
                                  which 320x240 RGB565 picture the screen is
                                  most like, and the share of its pixels
                                  that match; with --wait, until the best
                                  matches --min of them and is named by RE
  dev/emulate log [-f]            the session's output
  dev/emulate wait screen|exit [SECS]   until the screen settles, or the
  dev/emulate wait log REGEX [SECS]     session ends; or a log line matches
  dev/emulate status
  dev/emulate stop                (sudo, if the supervisor was killed)
  sudo dev/emulate exec [--for SECS] CMD [ARG ...]   runs CMD in the fake
                                  root (e.g. /mnt/neochumby/neochumby sensors)
  sudo dev/emulate selftest       checks the fake devices from this side
  sudo dev/emulate mkstick IMG [--size MB] [--force] [TREE]
                                  a FAT image, a new regular file (--force
                                  to overwrite one), with TREE as neochumby/
                                  if given (as tools/stick)

The emulator's directory is --state DIR, else $NEOCHUMBY_EMU, else
/var/tmp/neochumby-emulate-UID for the user who ran sudo. sudo drops
NEOCHUMBY_EMU, so pass it on (`sudo --preserve-env=NEOCHUMBY_EMU`) or use
--state. The directory is root's: root works in it only through a
descriptor of it, checked, so a user can't redirect what root does there.
Only `start`, `exec`, `selftest` and `mkstick` need root; the rest work as
the user who ran sudo.

The fake root, a tmpfs in a mount namespace of its own (so nothing is left
mounted when it stops):

  /mnt/neochumby      TREE, read-only, as a neochumbyd's NFS export
  /mnt/usb            the stick, else an empty directory
  /psp                STATE/psp, kept from run to run, like the flash
  /tmp                a 32 MB tmpfs, as on the Chumby
  /proc/sys/sense1    brightness, dimlevel and spkmute, in STATE/sense1; the
                      rest of /proc/sys is the PC's, read-only
  /dev/fb0            STATE/fb0, a 153,600-byte file; mmap(MAP_SHARED) of a
                      file works as it does on the device
  /dev/input/event0   the touchscreen: each open gets every event from then
                      on, as evdev does; writes are delivered as touches
  /dev/switch         the top button: each read returns its level, one byte;
                      one open at a time ("Resource busy"); writes fail
  /dev/dsp            a sink, paced to the rate set by its OSS ioctls, which
                      reports its buffer (GETOSPACE); one open at a time

event0, switch and dsp are served over FUSE by this script: a FIFO can't be
the touchscreen, because every reader (the session's corner watch and the
app in front) must see every event, and a regular file can't be the button,
whose level each read returns afresh.

The session runs as on the Chumby: as root, in a UTS namespace whose host
name is "chumby", with QEMU_CPU=arm926 so an instruction the ARM926 lacks
faults here too. It is root without the capabilities that would let it out
(mounts, chroot, modules, raw devices, the clock, network setup) or into the
PC's kernel settings, and parts of /proc are hidden as a container runtime
hides them. It shares the PC's network, so a neochumbyd on the PC can pair
with it (--server 127.0.0.1). Its output goes to STATE/log.
"""

import ctypes
import errno
import heapq
import json
import os
import re
import select
import shutil
import signal
import socket
import stat
